## 3. The Search for A and B

### 3.1 The Candidate Space

The parameters A and B are searched over exact framework expressions. The base quantities are:

$$d = 4,\ N = 64,\ H = 16,\ V = 48,\ kL = 38.442527,\ v_{EW} = 246.22$$

The candidate expressions are products of these quantities with rational coefficients:

$$A = \frac{p}{q} \cdot d^a \cdot N^b \cdot H^c \cdot V^e \cdot kL^f \cdot v_{EW}^g$$

$$B = \frac{p}{q} \cdot d^a \cdot N^b \cdot H^c \cdot V^e \cdot kL^f$$

with small integers p, q and small exponents.

### 3.2 The Search Space

| Parameter | Base quantities | Rational coefficients | Total candidates |
|:----------|:----------------|:----------------------|:-----------------|
| A | 21 | 36 | 756 |
| B | 26 | 15 | 392 |

Total (A, B) pairs: **756 × 392 = 296,352**.

### 3.3 The Test

For each (A, B) pair, the map

$$m_i = A \cdot \exp\left(-B \cdot \frac{N}{4}_i\right)$$

is computed for all 12 particles. The RMS log10 deviation from the observed masses is computed:

$$\text{RMS} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{12} \sum_i \left(\log_{10} m_i - \log_{10} m_i^{\text{obs}}\right)^2}$$

The pairs are ranked by RMS.

